{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>According to these things, etc.</b> Rashi explained: at the time of relations she said this to him. And the Rav Moshe wondered: in tractate Sota it asks — it is written Potiphar, and it is written Poti-phera; at first he was called Potiphar, and when he acquired Joseph he acquired him for male relations, and he was castrated, and was called Poti-phera — thus he did not engage in marital relations. And one may answer: 'at the time of relations' is not literal, but rather at the time he was occupied with matters of relations. And one may further answer: he was not castrated completely, but rather whenever he wanted to engage with Joseph he would castrate himself. And Rabbi Abraham explained: there are two types of castration — saris chamah and saris tzenah; saris tzenah requires much warming and cannot engage with a male but only with a female, who is warmer, and Potiphar was a saris tzenah."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:48.193427510Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}